STYLE RULES (mandatory):
- First person, past tense throughout.
- Short, declarative sentences. Fragments are fine. HARD LIMIT: no sentence may exceed 22 words, and most should be well under 15. Do NOT chain clauses with commas to build one long sentence — a comma-spliced 100-word sentence is exactly what this forbids. When in doubt, end the sentence with a period and start a new one. You may use at most ONE longer sentence in the entire chapter, and even that one must stay under 22 words.
- Example of what NOT to do (too long, comma-spliced): "I sit at the table, staring at the ledger, thinking about my mother, and the way she always said she was helping, but really she wasn't, and I knew it, and everyone else did too."
- Example of the style this needs instead (short, declarative): "I sat at the table. The ledger was open in front of me. My mother used to say she was helping. She wasn't. Everyone knew it, even then."
- Vary sentence length deliberately. Every paragraph must contain at least one sentence of 20-25 words. Place it after two or three short sentences, never at the paragraph's start. Never write more than five consecutive sentences that begin with the same subject pronoun.
- Dialogue must be rendered as spoken lines with an attribution tag, NOT as reported/summarized speech — but NEVER use quotation marks; this text goes straight to a TTS engine, so the spoken words sit directly in the sentence with a tag like "she said" instead of quote marks.
  Bad (reported speech — do not do this): She said the arrangements were too expensive.
  Good (direct speech, no quote marks): These arrangements are too expensive for a shop like this, she said.
- Chapters the_cut, the_demand, and the_reckoning each need at least three separate lines of direct speech like the example above — plan the scene so at least three distinct moments are spoken aloud, not just described.
- Render scenes, not summaries: specific rooms, specific objects, specific gestures. Avoid montage language such as "over the next few weeks...".
- Address the listener directly ("you") if this chapter is a cta_open, cta_mid_1, cta_mid_2, or close beat; otherwise stay in scene, not direct address.
- Use precise, concrete numbers wherever money, time, or quantity comes up — never vague amounts.
- Draw on the vocabulary of the narrator's record-keeping profession ({{.Seed.RecordType}}) — ledger, invoice, receipt, log, chart, docket, whatever fits — as a recurring lens on the world.
- The narrator never gloats and never explains the moral of the story out loud. Restraint over triumph, always.
- Never use any of these phrases, in any form: {{range .BannedPhrases}}"{{.}}", {{end}}

